{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>that he may give it to me, etc.</b> The verse was precise in using the language of gift, even though he took money from him, as it says ״for full silver,״ and also it was precise in saying <b>in the midst of you.</b> Now, Abraham acted wisely to acquire the cave in a manner that leaves no room for contestation afterward. And since our Sages of blessed memory said (Zohar vol. 1, 128a) that Ephron all his days never saw in the cave anything but darkness and confusion, if so he could come with a claim once he knows that the cave is precious in value, and he can even retract forever, and the ownership of the cave would not remain in Abraham's hand forever. And even though there is no claim of overreaching (ona'ah) for land, Abraham was concerned for the reasoning of the Jerusalem Talmud, which Tosafot wrote (Kiddushin 42b) that land does have overreaching in half its value — but specifically overreaching of one-sixth does not apply to them." "<b>Further,</b> there is room to be concerned that perhaps they may contest the sale after the fact on grounds of a neighbor's preemptive right (bar metzra), or that afterward a deed of encumbrance on that land may emerge and they will seize it from Abraham's hand. Therefore he acted wisely to rectify all this, and used the language of both gift and sale — the intention being to create a form that includes both, namely an exchange (chalifin), which partakes of sale and partakes of gift, where one may exchange a needle for a camel and there is no overreaching in any manner. And the law of exchange applies to land and to movable goods but not to coins; therefore they said of blessed memory in Bekhorot, and this is their language (Bekhorot 50a): Rabbi Chanina said, every shekel mentioned in the Torah is selas, etc., except for the shekels mentioned regarding Ephron, which are kantarin, overreaching applies to the merchant, merchandise goes out, etc. — thus far their language. And from this point on no room remained for Ephron to come with a claim. And regarding the concern of a neighbor's preemptive right and also the concern of an encumbrance, he made all the people of the city act as brokers in the matter, and thereby the claim of bar metzra was nullified, and it was also revealed that there was no encumbrance upon it, since the matter was in the ears of all who came to the gate of his city." "And the verse's saying <b>for a possession of a buryingplace</b> — since he asked of him only the cave, but the field still remained Ephron's property, there is room for him to say afterward that he does not want people trampling through it when they pass over it to bury the dead, for he sold it to him only to bury Sarah, and there is no right of foot-traffic through his field yet. Therefore he said <b>for a possession of a buryingplace.</b>"], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:43:01.175996747Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}